# A tab click opens that thread in the FOCUSED pane, and leaves the other one
# alone.
#
# This is the load-bearing half of "one global tab strip". A per-pane strip
# would double the chrome and halve the horizontal room the split exists to
# create, and this app's sidebar already carries the thread list -- so there is
# one strip, and what a click on it MEANS is "open this here", where here is
# wherever the keyboard is. If that turns out to be wrong it is wrong in a way
# somebody will report, and this is the script that will change.
#
# HOW IT IS ASSERTED, since this is the awkward part. Both threads are on
# screen at once while split, so "t9 is visible" cannot say WHICH pane got it.
# Closing the split can: closing leaves pane 0, so if the tab opened in the
# focused (right) pane, the surviving pane is still on t2 -- and if it had
# opened in the left pane instead, the survivor would be t9. One assertion,
# and it fails in the exact way the wrong behaviour would produce.
#
# settings: {"window_width":1100,"window_height":760,"open_tabs":["t2","t9"],"active_tab":"t2","theme":"dark"}

require_thread t2

# One pane on t2.
assert_ui transcript_scroll hidden=false
expect_text "Reconcile this cycle's Stars payouts against the ledger"
expect_no_text "Recurring: kick the pending queue every ten minutes."

# Split. Both panes show t2 -- the cheapest useful default, and the reason the
# text below is still t2's.
key CTRL+BACKSLASH
wait_frames 20
assert_ui split_right hidden=false
assert_ui split_focus_edge x=280

# Put the keyboard in the right pane.
click 900 400
wait_frames 10
assert_ui split_focus_edge x=693

# Click the t9 tab. Tabs are 224 wide from x=285 and 33 tall from y=33, so
# (600, 48) is the middle of the second one -- clear of the first tab's close
# button at x=480, which is where a first guess at this landed and which
# closed t2 instead. By coordinate rather than by name because the harness
# cannot name one tab of a strip (afterhours_gaps.md #55).
click 600 48
wait_frames 30
require_thread t9

# Both threads are on screen: the click opened one WITHOUT closing the other.
expect_text "Recurring: kick the pending queue every ten minutes."
expect_text "Reconcile this cycle's Stars payouts against the ledger"

# And it went into the pane that had the keyboard. Close the split -- pane 0
# is what survives -- and t2 is what is left, which is only true if t9 never
# touched the left pane.
key CTRL+BACKSLASH
wait_frames 30
assert_ui transcript_scroll w=820
expect_text "Reconcile this cycle's Stars payouts against the ledger"
expect_no_text "Recurring: kick the pending queue every ten minutes."

# The other direction, so this is a rule and not an accident of which pane is
# which: split again, leave the keyboard in the LEFT pane, click t9's tab, and
# now it is the left pane that changed.
key CTRL+BACKSLASH
wait_frames 20
assert_ui split_focus_edge x=280
click 600 48
wait_frames 30
key CTRL+BACKSLASH
wait_frames 30
expect_text "Recurring: kick the pending queue every ten minutes."
expect_no_text "Reconcile this cycle's Stars payouts against the ledger"
